  

<h2 id="plus-how-to-learn-when-youve-started-to-believe-the-lie-that-you-cant">Plus how to learn when you’ve started to believe the lie that you can’t</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/3ea803bc-4914-4f9b-9e93-b66a97a1b4e3/pasted-image-20261109160717.png" title="Ink sketch of a stick figure doing a double take as they first say something (with specifically squiggled lines inside a speech bubble) and then look at a robot box sitting on a table next to them that is saying exactly the same thing." alt="Ink sketch of a stick figure doing a double take as they first say something (with specifically squiggled lines inside a speech bubble) and then look at a robot box sitting on a table next to them that is saying exactly the same thing." width="2100" height="1316"></p>
<p>This week I take a look at accessibility issues and solutions for people with one or more disabilities. Things aren’t always as they seem, for bad and for good!</p>
<hr>
<h3 id="autism-and-ai">Autism and <span class="small-caps">AI</span></h3>
<p>There’s a new acronym floating around, based on<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>tl:dr” (too long, didn’t read). It’s<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>ai:dr”. Clever, sure, but also problematic. Maybe you <em>are</em> pretty good at sniffing out AI-written content, but I bet you don’t get it right 100% of the time.</p>
<p><a href="https://atomicpoet.org/notice/BBvKkAj098WplAQ8bw" target="_blank">Chris Trottier</a> talks about the accessibility advantages of artificial intelligence for the autistic community, and how defending human intelligence can stray into dodgy territory.</p>
<p><em><span class="pull-double">“</span>No matter how much you dislike <span class="small-caps">AI</span>, there’s still a huge difference between criticizing it and policing how humans are permitted to communicate. Because if your defence of human authenticity requires autistic people to prove they’re human, something has gone seriously wrong.”</em></p>
<p>Some autistic people naturally write like <span class="small-caps">AI</span> — whoops! I mean, <span class="small-caps">AI</span> has learned to write using patterns that come naturally to some autistic people.</p>
<p>The best way I can think of to tell which is which is for the humans of the internet to make more real connections, and vouch for each other. Let’s make chains of real people who create delightfully flawed and occasionally brilliant work! If you have a blog, I encourage you to create a <code>/friends</code> page. <a href="https://ellanew.com/friends" class="wikilink">Here’s mine</a>.</p>
<p><a href="https://powrss.com/" target="_blank">Powrss</a> can show you a ton of real people if you ever feel like you’re running short of them. There’s an addictive button there <a href="https://powrss.com/random" target="_blank">here</a> that will send you through space to the writings of a random wonderful human.</p>
<h3 id="new-ways-to-learn-for-multiply-disabled-people">New ways to learn for multiply-disabled people</h3>
<p>Lanie, who is blind and autistic, is the driving force behind<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span><a href="https://superablelearning.com/multabilities/courses/more-than-one-way-in" target="_blank">More Than One Way In</a><span class="push-double"></span><span class="pull-double">”</span>; a succinct, 6-module course designed to help people who have multiple disabilities to discover that they <em>can</em> learn, and different ways to do it. It’s also for anyone who has ever felt that the learning methods they’ve experienced were not right for them.</p>
<p>Each module only takes a few minutes. I finished the whole thing in one sitting and loved it!</p>
<p>I am (mostly) a regularly-abled person, but still found things in each module that opened my mind and spoke directly to my heart. Lanie is looking for feedback. You can use <a href="https://join.slack.com/t/multabilities/shared_invite/zt-4fvd9qnwx-gUoJneFkId9CSBmAVCQcog" target="_blank">this Slack invite</a> (valid until Dec 5) to have your say, or contact Lanie directly on <a href="https://ellanew.com/Posts/2026/2026-PTPL/@RareBird15@allovertheplace.ca">Mastodon</a>.</p>
